crypto-ffi: backup primitives of Otaku, processed to-device events

Additions only, against tag matrix-sdk-crypto-0.19.1 (commit
b18166c68bb958a21f0bca8b2d8320cb53583362), after 0001 to 0003. No behaviour
of an existing call changes. Otaku ADR-0034 (D-379) and ADR-0033 §3.

1. Backup primitives (src/backup_primitives.rs), free functions without an
   Olm machine nor a store, for the backup envelopes of Otaku on Android
   (the web composes the same envelopes from libsodium):
   - backup_argon2id(password, salt, kdf_set): Argon2id v1.3, one lane,
     32-byte output; closed list of parameter sets written here (1 = t 3,
     m 65 536 KiB; 2 = t 3, m 131 072 KiB), salt of 16 bytes, password of
     1 to 1 024 bytes. The work memory is reserved with try_reserve_exact
     (a refused allocation is an error, not an abort), given to
     hash_password_into_with_memory and erased before it is freed.
   - backup_seal / backup_open: XChaCha20-Poly1305 IETF, 16-byte tag
     appended (libsodium layout); the nonce comes from the caller.
   - backup_blake2b256(data): unkeyed BLAKE2b, 32-byte output.
   Fixed error codes (BackupPrimitiveError): argon2_params, aead_input,
   aead_open_failed, input_too_large, insufficient_memory, internal. Inputs,
   keys and plaintexts are erased on return (Zeroizing), failure included.
   New dependencies, pinned, no default features: argon2 =0.6.0 (zeroize
   only: `alloc` would add password-hash and phc to the lock through its
   weak feature), blake2 =0.11.0 (zeroize), chacha20poly1305 =0.11.0 (alloc,
   zeroize; already in the tree through matrix-sdk-store-encryption, which
   now also gets its zeroize feature). Cargo.lock gains argon2 and blake2
   only (base64ct, cpufeatures 0.3.0, digest 0.11.2 and zeroize are already
   locked).
   Tests (cargo test -p matrix-sdk-crypto-ffi --lib backup_primitives):
   RFC 9106 §5.3, RFC 7693 appendix A, draft-irtf-cfrg-xchacha A.3.1, the
   three envelope fixtures of the Otaku repository (fixtures/otaku/, same
   bytes checked by SHA-256, including a valid case of set 2 and the
   largest envelope), refusals and bounds, erasure of the work memory, and
   the exported surface (kdf_set is the only cost parameter).

   Security updates in Cargo.lock, all semver-compatible with the
   workspace requirements, checksums from the crates.io index: vodozemac
   0.11.0 -> 0.11.2 (RUSTSEC-2026-0510, -0445, -0451; it enables the
   `zeroize` feature of aes, cbc, cipher, hmac, sha2 and chacha20poly1305,
   hence the new `zeroize` edges of aes, cipher and hybrid-array), ctutils
   0.4.2 -> 0.4.4 (RUSTSEC-2026-0385), rustix 1.1.4 -> 1.1.6
   (RUSTSEC-2026-0486), tokio 1.53.1 -> 1.53.3 (RUSTSEC-2026-0386).

2. SyncChangesResult.processed_to_device_events (new field; the existing
   to_device_events is unchanged): for each to-device event, Decrypted
   { raw, sender, sender_device, sender_curve25519_key, verification_state }
   (verification_state = "verified" or a fixed name of
   verification_level_name of 0003, now pub(crate)), UnableToDecrypt
   { reason } (fixed code), PlainText { raw } or Invalid. Sending a custom
   encrypted to-device event needs no change: the existing
   create_encrypted_to_device_request takes the share strategy
   (CollectStrategy::IdentityBasedStrategy for Otaku).

Not compiled locally (no Rust toolchain on the workstation): the build
workflow is the first compilation.

